# Week 8 — Quality Requirements and Prioritisation

## 1. Quality Requirements
This week I turned vague quality statements into measurable candidate requirements for the Community Volunteer and Event Coordination System. All targets are **proposed** and need confirmation from stakeholders.

ID | Statement | Type | Priority | Source | Rationale | Verification 

NFR01 | In a usability test with representative first-time volunteers, at least 4 out of 5 shall find an event and apply for a role within 3 minutes without assistance. | Usability | Should (provisional) | [PROPOSED — confirm with stakeholders] | Volunteers may have varied digital skills; signing up should not need help. | Task-based usability test. Depends on FR08 and FR09. |
NFR02 | With 25 concurrent users, at least 95% of event searches shall display results within 3 seconds. | Performance | Should (provisional) | [PROPOSED — confirm with stakeholders] | Slow search discourages volunteers from browsing events. | Timed test on a sample event list; record the time between the search request and the displayed results; at least 95 of 100 attempts within 3 s. |
NFR03 | Volunteer phone numbers and email addresses shall be visible only to authorised users who need them for their role. | Privacy / Security | Must (provisional) | [PROPOSED — confirm with stakeholders] | Contact details are personal data. The scenario says privacy needs consideration. | Access-control test with different accounts (volunteer, organiser, unauthorised user). Who counts as authorised is an open question. |
NFR04 | All controls in the application journey (browse, select role, apply) shall be operable using the keyboard only. | Accessibility | Should (provisional) | [PROPOSED — confirm with stakeholders] | The scenario says accessibility needs consideration. | Complete the journey with the keyboard only; focus stays visible. This is not a full WCAG 2.2 assessment. |

The numbers in NFR01 and NFR02 are examples, not agreed targets. NFR03 could also be a functional requirement with a security rationale. I classify it as Privacy / Security because it limits who can see the data.

## 2. MoSCoW Prioritisation
These priorities are provisional and need stakeholder confirmation.

Priority | Items | Reason / workaround 

Must | FR01 Register as volunteer | Without accounts there are no volunteers in the system. |
Must | FR02 Record skills and availability | Needed to match volunteers to roles. |
Must | FR04 Create event and roles; FR12 Edit or cancel event | Without events and roles there is nothing to apply for. |
Must (provisional) | FR08 View events; FR09 Apply for a role | The volunteer's main task. FR09 is still a candidate pending confirmation. |
Should | FR05 Role suitability; FR10 Required skills on a role | Useful for matching. An organiser could check by hand as a workaround. How skills are checked is unknown. |
Should | FR06 Event change notifications | Valuable; organisers could contact volunteers directly as a workaround. |
Should | FR07 Attendance list | Useful for organisers; a spreadsheet could be used temporarily. |
could | FR03 Upload evidence, FR11 Export, waitlist and calendar view (candidates) | Not supported by the scenario; decisions needed. |
Won't (this release) | None assigned | No release exclusions have been confirmed. |

## 3. Prototype Updates
The Week 7 screens show "Application sent — this is not a confirmed place" (Screen 5). This helps the volunteer understand that submitting is not approval. I redrew the screens to remove details that are not in the scenario. The prototype is used to explore requirements, not to show how they will be built.

## 4. Open Questions
# | Question | Who can answer |

1 | What search response time and load do stakeholders expect? | Event Organiser, IT Support |
2 | How should usability be measured, and who are the representative volunteers? | Volunteers, Event Organiser |
3 | Who checks volunteers' skills or experience? | Event Organiser |
4 | What should happen when a volunteer does not have the skills or experience a role needs? | Event Organiser |
5 | Who counts as an authorised user for volunteer contact details? | Privacy role |

## 5. Reflection
This week I learned to turn words like easy and fast into clear statements with an operation, conditions, a threshold and a check. I also gave each priority a reason. I noticed that matching volunteers to roles depends on how skills are checked, which is still unknown, so I marked it provisional. Next, I will confirm the targets and priorities with stakeholders before treating them as agreed.
